; MAC-TOOL-5: a machine-applicable fix is suppressed inside an
; expansion, and only inside one.
;
; A diagnostic reported from generated code is anchored at the
; INVOCATION - the line the author can change (MAC-DIAG-5) - and that
; is exactly what makes its `~>` replacement wrong: the replacement
; was computed against text the author's file does not contain, so
; applying it pastes it over the macro call. Measured before the rule:
; `bumpIt` below offered `~>"mut tmp.0"` across the bytes of
; `(bumpIt 3)`, and `tmp.0` is a renamed binder no source can spell
; (MAC-HYG-3a) - the AXDL `~>` field is consumed by tools that apply
; it without asking.
;
; Six diagnostics, in pairs, so the golden shows the rule and its
; boundary in one file:
;
;   1  AX3012 inside an expansion - help TEXT kept, `~>` gone, frame
;      naming `bumpIt`
;   2  AX3012 written by hand - `~>"mut tmp"` intact
;   3  AX3001 inside an expansion - the did-you-mean help kept, `~>`
;      gone, frame naming `callIt`
;   4  AX3001 written by hand - `~>"helper"` intact
;   5  AX3012 inside a MODULE-side expansion (BumpMod.ax) - the same
;      rule where the invocation is not in this file at all. It needed
;      two fixes to hold: the phase-D frame carried site 0, so it
;      could not join a diagnostic that knew its own module, and
;      without the join nothing disarmed the fix
;   6  AX3012 written by hand IN THE MODULE - keeps its `~>`, and
;      names BumpMod.ax. It reported against THIS file's bytes until
;      the two AX3012 emitters stamped the unit like every other
;      emitter in the checker: a three-line entry file was told its
;      own line 4 was at fault
(import BumpMod)

(macro (bumpIt v) (let ((tmp 1))
  {
    (set tmp 2)
    (+ tmp v)
  }
))

(macro (callIt x) (helperr x))

(:: helper (-> Int Int))

(fn (helper y) y)

(:: fromMacro (-> Int Int))

(fn (fromMacro n) (+ (bumpIt n) (callIt n)))

(:: byHand (-> Int Int))

(fn (byHand n)
  (let ((tmp 1))
    {
      (set tmp 2)
      (+ tmp (helperr n))
    }
  )
)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (+ (+ (fromMacro 1) (byHand 2)) (+ (bumpThing One) (handBump One))))
